---
import { stages } from "../../data/surveillance.js";
const scene = stages[0];
---

<section id="surveillance" class="chapter dark">
  <div class="chapter-heading reveal">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span class="orange">01 / THE WATCHING CITY</span><span
        >COLLECTION → CONNECTION → CONSEQUENCE</span
      >
    </div><h2>A camera sees a car.<br />A network sees <em>a life.</em></h2><p>
      A useful investigation tool can become a much broader source of power. The
      question changes as the system grows.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="city-experiment" data-story>
    <div class="scene" data-scene="0">
      <img
        src="/assets/street.webp"
        alt="Conceptual illustration of a generic security camera overlooking a lone pedestrian in a city at night"
        width="1536"
        height="1024"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <div class="scene-shade"></div>
      <svg
        class="scene-network"
        viewBox="0 0 600 800"
        fill="none"
        aria-hidden="true"
      >
        <g class="network-collection">
          <path
            d="M160 170h-35v35 M300 170h35v35 M125 305v35h35 M300 340h35v-35"
          ></path>
          <circle cx="230" cy="255" r="7"></circle>
        </g>
        <g class="network-connections">
          <path
            pathLength="1"
            d="M230 255L450 140L485 355L350 440L105 450L230 255L350 440M230 255L485 355"
          ></path>
          <circle cx="450" cy="140" r="7"></circle><circle
            cx="485"
            cy="355"
            r="7"></circle>
          <circle cx="350" cy="440" r="7"></circle><circle
            cx="105"
            cy="450"
            r="7"></circle>
        </g>
        <g class="network-consequence">
          <path pathLength="1" d="M350 440L465 525H530"></path>
          <rect x="430" y="490" width="115" height="70" rx="2"></rect>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <div class="scene-top">
        <span class="scene-tag">ILLUSTRATIVE SCENARIO</span><span
          id="scene-step">{`01 / ${scene.name}`}</span
        >
      </div>
      <div class="scene-text">
        <p id="scene-kicker">{scene.kicker}</p>
        <h3 id="scene-title" set:html={scene.title} />
        <p id="scene-description">{scene.description}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="scene-footer">
        Conceptual illustration & diagram · Not a Flock installation
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="experiment-panel">
      <div class="story-controls">
        <div class="stages" role="group" aria-label="Surveillance stage">
          {
            ["Collect", "Connect", "Act"].map((label, index) => (
              <button
                data-stage={index}
                aria-pressed={index === 0 ? "true" : "false"}
              >
                <span>{`0${index + 1}`}</span>
                {label}
              </button>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <div class="choice" role="group" aria-label="Oversight condition">
          <button data-choice="0" aria-pressed="true"
            >Operator discretion</button
          >
          <button data-choice="1" aria-pressed="false">Independent check</button
          >
        </div>
        <button
          id="compare"
          class="text-button"
          aria-expanded="false"
          aria-controls="story-beats"
          >See both conditions together <span>↗</span></button
        >
        <span class="sr-only" role="status" id="story-status"></span>
      </div>
      <div id="story-beats">
        {
          stages.map((item, index) => (
            <article
              class="story-beat"
              id={`story-stage-${index}`}
              data-beat={index}
              aria-labelledby={`story-question-${index}`}
            >
              <p class="beat-number">{`0${index + 1} / ${item.name}`}</p>
              <h3 id={`story-question-${index}`}>{item.question}</h3>
              <p class="beat-description">{item.description}</p>
              <div class="beat-results">
                {item.conditions.map((result, conditionIndex) => (
                  <div
                    class="stage-result"
                    data-condition={conditionIndex}
                    hidden={conditionIndex !== 0}
                  >
                    <span class="result-label">
                      {conditionIndex
                        ? "INDEPENDENT CHECK"
                        : "OPERATOR DISCRETION"}
                    </span>
                    <h4
                      id={
                        index === 0 && conditionIndex === 0
                          ? "result-title"
                          : undefined
                      }
                      data-result-title
                    >
                      {result.title}
                    </h4>
                    <p>{result.benefit}</p>
                    <div class="tradeoff">
                      <span class="result-label">
                        WHAT IT ASKS US TO ACCEPT
                      </span>
                      <p data-result-cost>{result.cost}</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="affected">
                      <span class="result-label">WHO FEELS THE DIFFERENCE</span>
                      <p>{result.people}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <details class="evidence">
    <summary
      ><span><span class="orange">↳</span> Ground this in the real world</span
      ><span class="summary-hint"
        >Flock, facial recognition & the limits of accuracy</span
      ><span class="plus">+</span></summary
    ><div class="evidence-content">
      <div>
        <h3>Useful does not settle legitimate.</h3><p>
          Flock offers automated licence plate recognition and a national LPR
          network. Its <a
            href="https://www.flocksafety.com/trust"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">trust materials</a
          > describe its approach to privacy and customer controls. Those are the
          provider’s account of its safeguards, rather than independent proof of how
          every deployment operates.
        </p><p>
          The scenario above is a general policy comparison, not a claim that
          every Flock customer lacks these checks. Plate recognition and facial
          recognition are different technologies. Both raise questions about who
          can search, for which purposes, and with what recourse.
        </p>
      </div><div>
        <h3>A documented failure.</h3><p>
          Robert Williams was wrongfully arrested in Detroit in 2020 after
          police relied on an incorrect facial-recognition result. The 2024
          settlement introduced requirements for independent supporting evidence
          and an audit of past cases. The <a
            href="https://www.aclu.org/press-releases/civil-rights-advocates-achieve-the-nations-strongest-police-department-policy-on-facial-recognition-technology"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">ACLU’s account and settlement links</a
          > come from his legal representatives.
        </p><p>
          Our inference: a safeguard has to govern how a result becomes an
          action. Better matching alone cannot decide whether a use is
          justified.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>
  <div class="chapter-takeaway">
    <span>THE CHECK THAT MATTERS</span><p>
      Ask whether a use is justified<br /><em>before it becomes ordinary.</em>
    </p><a href="#obligation-6">Obligation 06 / Give people recourse ↗</a>
  </div>
</section>
